# ═══ proof 08 — for real: the agent builds a chess game and plays it ═══════════
# Run: OPENCODE_API_KEY=… bash scripts/kn9t-demo-rec scripts/demos/real-chess.seq --no-policy
# MiMo v2.6 Pro, its reasoning stream visible, the plugin-creation skill in the
# repo. A real mission brief — explicit deliverables, no micromanaging: the
# model decides its own steps. game.md is logged as play goes, so the film
# always reaches the final answer.
config opencode-go.config.toml

rate 25
pause 1.5
say kn9t
pause 5.0
sh mkdir -p $WORK/.agents/skills && cp -r /home/emericclement/dev/kn9t/.agents/skills/kn9t-plugin-creation $WORK/.agents/skills/
pause 1.5
key ctrl+p
pause 1.2
type think
pause 0.8
send
pause 1.5
say Read .agents/skills/kn9t-plugin-creation/SKILL.md first — it documents the plugin protocol you need. Then build me a kn9t plugin, chess.py, to play chess against the machine: it must register a Lua TUI view drawing the 8x8 chessboard with unicode pieces and coordinates, expose a tool chess_move {move} (long algebraic, like e2e4) that validates and plays WHITE's move and answers with the resulting board, and answer every white move automatically from black with a random legal move inside the plugin. Simple piece movement rules are enough — no castling, no en passant. When it is written, hot-load it into the running server with one curl and play a short game as white. Log every move in game.md as you go, one line per ply, and finish by telling me how the game went.
pause 55.0
wait-file $WORK/chess.py 900
wait-idle 900
pause 25.0
key F1
pause 3.5
key F1
pause 1.0
click 60 45
pause 3.5
click 30 30
pause 3.5
click 30 30
pause 1.5
key ctrl+q
